# A figure's proportions, measured in heads on the artist's wooden figure, and the quick gesture
# drawing that starts from one line of action through the body.
lesson art-figures-in-proportion-and-motion v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=2 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Figures in proportion and in motion"
  goal "Draw a person in proportion by measuring in heads, and catch a moving pose quickly with a line of action."

  look {
    scene 23x13 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        layfigure stand heads=7.5 lines=true action=false
        layfigure run heads=7.5 pose="run" lines=false action=true
      }
    }
    say """
      At the waterfall gorge Ada sketches the people crossing the rope bridge and working at the mill.
      Artists measure a person in heads. A grown-up is about seven and a half heads tall, with the
      elbows level with the waist, the wrists at the top of the legs and the knees halfway down the
      legs. A child's head is a bigger share of the body. To draw someone moving there is no time to
      measure, so a gesture drawing starts with one line of action, from the head to the foot that
      takes the weight, and hangs the body on it in thirty seconds.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.fig-count count=1 seed=1
    level easy {
      worked art.fig-head-size k=0 s=6 q=0 level=medium
    }
    practice art.fig-head-size count=2 seed=5
    practice art.fig-landmark count=2 seed=1
    practice art.fig-mistake count=1 hard-count=2 seed=3
    practice art.fig-gesture count=2 seed=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.fig-halfway-why r=1 easy-r=0 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.fig-gestures
  }
  remember {
    say "Measure a still figure in heads; catch a moving one with its line of action first."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wooden figure in the drawings is the jointed lay figure artists have posed for centuries.
    Most of the questions are marked by the machine. The first counts a figure's heads and matches
    it to an age. The core works out a head's size from a height in heads, which is ratio met in a
    drawing, places the joints of a standing grown-up, finds a proportion drawn wrong, and matches a
    pose to its line of action. The proportions are averages: real people differ, and the lesson
    says "about" for that reason.

    Watch for a child who draws the arms too short, with the hands at the waist; it is the commonest
    mistake at every age, and letting the arms hang beside their own body and looking where the
    wrists come settles it. If the head sums are stuck, stack coins or blocks, one a head, to the
    figure's height. The two-star question asks why a small child's middle is higher, which is the
    reason proportions are learnt rather than guessed. The gestures and the measured figure are the
    three-star question and are responded to rather than marked. Off the page, take turns to hold a
    pose for thirty seconds while the other draws.

    Respond by pointing to a line of action: "This line leans right over, and I can feel the run."
    Ask which pose was hardest to catch in thirty seconds.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the figures to match are a baby of one and a grown-up, a head is worked once in the teacher's pen before more grown-ups, the joints are the elbows, wrists and knees, the mistakes are short arms or a big head, the poses are running, bending and walking, the two-star child is one, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the figure to match can be a child of eleven, the heads are a child's six and a half and the height is asked from the head, the joints include the eyes and fingertips, a second mistake is added and high knees appear, the poses include balancing on a stepping stone and reaching up to the bridge rope, the two-star child is three, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
